  "content": "# Bounded LRU Cache in Python Using `collections.OrderedDict`\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a data structure that maintains a fixed-size collection of key-value pairs. When the cache becomes full, the item that has not been accessed for the longest time (the \"least recently used\" item) is evicted. This strategy is effective because it exploits the principle of temporal locality: items accessed recently are likely to be accessed again.\n\nPython\u2019s `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation for implementing an LRU cache because it preserves insertion order and supports moving items to the end in O(1) time. This tutorial walks through a complete, production-quality implementation, including edge cases, tests, a worked example, and complexity analysis.\n\n---\n\n## The Implementation\n\n### Key Design Decisions\n\n1. **Insertion order tracks recency**: When a key is accessed (via `get` or `put`), it is moved to the end of the `OrderedDict`, marking it as the most recently used.\n2. **Eviction is from the front**: The least recently used item is always at the front of the `OrderedDict`. We remove it with `popitem(last=False)`.\n3. **`get` updates recency**: Accessing a key moves it to the end, so frequently accessed items stay in the cache.\n4. **`put` handles three cases**: \n   - Key exists \u2192 update value and move to end.\n   - Key does not exist and cache is not full \u2192 insert at end.\n   - Key does not exist and cache is full \u2192 evict the front item, then insert at end.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded Least Recently Used (LRU) cache implemented using\n    collections.OrderedDict.\n\n    - get(key): Returns the value associated with `key` if present,\n      and marks the key as most recently used. Returns None if not found.\n    - put(key, value): Inserts or updates a key-value pair. If the cache\n      is at capacity, the least recently used entry is evicted.\n    - Capacity is defined at construction and is immutable.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be a positive integer.\")\n        self._capacity = capacity\n",
